1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:02,719
Bhai pressed the button to pay the bill,

2
00:00:02,719 --> 00:00:05,049
and the debt still went up.

3
00:00:05,049 --> 00:00:08,157
The button was called minimum due. He thought,

4
00:00:08,157 --> 00:00:10,099
today's work is done.

5
00:00:10,099 --> 00:00:13,594
In truth, that very night a meter started running.

6
00:00:13,594 --> 00:00:17,090
That meter kept running month after month, completely silently.

7
00:00:17,090 --> 00:00:20,262
Late at night, Bhai is looking at his phone in bed.

8
00:00:20,262 --> 00:00:23,786
The card app is open, with the bill in red at the top.

9
00:00:23,786 --> 00:00:26,606
Below are the buttons, total due and minimum due.

10
00:00:26,606 --> 00:00:29,425
The minimum due amount looks very small.

11
00:00:29,425 --> 00:00:31,540
Bhai smiles and presses that one.

12
00:00:31,540 --> 00:00:34,591
A few months later, Behen looks at his statement.

13
00:00:34,591 --> 00:00:37,260
He hasn't made any new spending at all.

14
00:00:37,260 --> 00:00:41,073
Still, the bill is bigger than before. Behen asks,

15
00:00:41,073 --> 00:00:45,267
you did pay, didn't you? Bhai says, yes, every month,

16
00:00:45,267 --> 00:00:46,030
on time.

17
00:00:46,030 --> 00:00:48,086
So some questions come up here.

18
00:00:48,086 --> 00:00:51,170
What really happens when you pay only the minimum due?

19
00:00:51,170 --> 00:00:53,569
Why does the bill not finish for years?

20
00:00:53,569 --> 00:00:56,996
And why is card interest so much heavier than other loans?

21
00:00:56,996 --> 00:01:00,080
Today we will understand these through a story, in simple language.

22
00:01:00,080 --> 00:01:03,025
And at the end, we will also see the way out.

23
00:01:03,025 --> 00:01:05,643
Because this trap is not only Bhai's.

24
00:01:05,643 --> 00:01:08,261
It's the festive sale season, phone, TV, fridge,

25
00:01:08,261 --> 00:01:10,225
everything is going on the card.

26
00:01:10,225 --> 00:01:13,170
So this story may be about your home too.

27
00:01:13,170 --> 00:01:16,178
Let's start from the beginning. A festive sale was on.

28
00:01:16,178 --> 00:01:18,584
Bhai's old phone had become slow.

29
00:01:18,584 --> 00:01:21,592
He saw a new phone online, big discount, plus a card offer on top.

30
00:01:21,592 --> 00:01:24,900
He thought, let me buy it now, I'll pay later.

31
00:01:24,900 --> 00:01:26,609
Up to here, everything was fine.

32
00:01:26,609 --> 00:01:29,343
A credit card is really a loan from the bank.

33
00:01:29,343 --> 00:01:32,761
You spend today, the bank pays the shopkeeper.

34
00:01:32,761 --> 00:01:35,496
At the end of the month, the bank sends a statement.

35
00:01:35,496 --> 00:01:38,230
A statement means the account of the whole month's spending.

36
00:01:38,230 --> 00:01:39,885
Now understand one good thing.

37
00:01:39,885 --> 00:01:43,527
If you pay the full bill on time, there is no interest.

38
00:01:43,527 --> 00:01:46,838
This is called the interest-free period, meaning time of free credit.

39
00:01:46,838 --> 00:01:50,480
Card companies give this period of 20 to 50 days.

40
00:01:50,480 --> 00:01:50,806
Just think,

41
00:01:50,806 --> 00:01:54,388
The bank is giving you free money for about 50 days.

42
00:01:54,388 --> 00:01:56,994
No interest, no fees. There is just one condition:

43
00:01:56,994 --> 00:01:58,948
you must pay the full bill on time.

44
00:01:58,948 --> 00:02:02,530
That condition is the key to the whole game, remember it.

45
00:02:02,530 --> 00:02:05,933
Then the statement came. The bill was as much as the price of the phone.

46
00:02:05,933 --> 00:02:08,201
Salary was still a while away,

47
00:02:08,201 --> 00:02:10,847
and the house rent had to be paid too.

48
00:02:10,847 --> 00:02:13,872
The app showed an easy way out: minimum due.

49
00:02:13,872 --> 00:02:16,140
Just a small part of the bill.

50
00:02:16,140 --> 00:02:18,261
Minimum due means that small amount,

51
00:02:18,261 --> 00:02:21,088
which, if you pay it, the bank won't call you a defaulter.

52
00:02:21,088 --> 00:02:24,622
No late fee will be charged, and the account keeps running. Bhai felt,

53
00:02:24,622 --> 00:02:28,510
the bank itself is offering this option, so it must be safe.

54
00:02:28,510 --> 00:02:31,660
And the logic makes sense too. Papa always used to say,

55
00:02:31,660 --> 00:02:34,810
pay your installments on time, your record will stay good. Bhai thought,

56
00:02:34,810 --> 00:02:37,330
I am paying the minimum on time too.

57
00:02:37,330 --> 00:02:40,480
I'll pay the rest next month. What's wrong with that?

58
00:02:40,480 --> 00:02:43,523
What's wrong is what the app doesn't write in big letters.

59
00:02:43,523 --> 00:02:47,243
The moment you don't pay the full bill, the free credit period ends.

60
00:02:47,243 --> 00:02:50,963
Interest is charged not just on the remaining amount, but on the whole bill.

61
00:02:50,963 --> 00:02:53,330
And often from the very day of purchase.

62
00:02:53,330 --> 00:02:57,462
So what Bhai thought turned out the opposite. He had thought,

63
00:02:57,462 --> 00:02:59,716
a little interest would be charged on the remaining amount.

64
00:02:59,716 --> 00:03:03,472
In fact, you don't get a free period even on new spending.

65
00:03:03,472 --> 00:03:05,726
Until the old bill is cleared,

66
00:03:05,726 --> 00:03:07,980
every new swipe gets interest right away.

67
00:03:07,980 --> 00:03:11,304
Now the next question. Why is the card's interest so heavy?

68
00:03:11,304 --> 00:03:14,628
For that, let's first understand Papa's PPF way of thinking.

69
00:03:14,628 --> 00:03:16,291
Because that is our normal,

70
00:03:16,291 --> 00:03:18,950
by which we measure every interest rate.

71
00:03:18,950 --> 00:03:22,198
Papa has a PPF passbook in his cupboard.

72
00:03:22,198 --> 00:03:26,258
PPF gives about 7.1 percent interest per year.

73
00:03:26,258 --> 00:03:29,912
Papa considers this a good return. Now think the opposite.

74
00:03:29,912 --> 00:03:33,160
On a card, you don't earn interest, you pay it.

75
00:03:33,160 --> 00:03:36,967
And the card's interest is many times the PPF interest.

76
00:03:36,967 --> 00:03:40,139
In the statement, it is written per month.

77
00:03:40,139 --> 00:03:43,312
A small percent per month sounds light.

78
00:03:43,312 --> 00:03:45,850
Add it up over a year and your eyes open.

79
00:03:45,850 --> 00:03:48,134
On top of that, this interest is compounding.

80
00:03:48,134 --> 00:03:51,722
Compounding means interest on interest. The interest that was charged this month,

81
00:03:51,722 --> 00:03:54,006
It got added to the next month's bill.

82
00:03:54,006 --> 00:03:56,616
Next month, interest will be charged on that bigger bill.

83
00:03:56,616 --> 00:03:58,900
The bill swells up a little every month.

84
00:03:58,900 --> 00:04:02,885
Think of debt as a bucket that you want to empty.

85
00:04:02,885 --> 00:04:05,783
Every month, you take out one mug of water.

86
00:04:05,783 --> 00:04:09,767
But the tap is open from above, the interest tap. The more you take out,

87
00:04:09,767 --> 00:04:13,390
the more comes back. The bucket never gets empty.

88
00:04:13,390 --> 00:04:15,400
And the story isn't over yet.

89
00:04:15,400 --> 00:04:18,415
The government also charges GST on interest and fees.

90
00:04:18,415 --> 00:04:20,760
GST means the tax charged on services.

91
00:04:20,760 --> 00:04:24,110
On card interest and fees, it is 18 percent.

92
00:04:24,110 --> 00:04:26,120
So, tax on top of interest too.

93
00:04:26,120 --> 00:04:30,292
Now read the lines on Bhai's statement. Finance charges, meaning interest.

94
00:04:30,292 --> 00:04:33,705
Below that, GST. Sometimes late fees, and then GST on that too.

95
00:04:33,705 --> 00:04:37,877
Bhai thought he was paying off the price of the phone.

96
00:04:37,877 --> 00:04:41,290
In reality, he was paying interest and tax.

97
00:04:41,290 --> 00:04:43,439
Then one month his salary came late.

98
00:04:43,439 --> 00:04:46,508
He missed the minimum due too. Now a late fee was charged,

99
00:04:46,508 --> 00:04:49,578
GST was charged on it, and the interest was already running.

100
00:04:49,578 --> 00:04:52,340
A small delay made the bill even bigger.

101
00:04:52,340 --> 00:04:54,211
And one more thing happens quietly.

102
00:04:54,211 --> 00:04:57,329
Late payment gets recorded in your credit score.

103
00:04:57,329 --> 00:04:59,824
Credit score means your borrowing report card.

104
00:04:59,824 --> 00:05:02,631
Tomorrow, if you want to take a home loan or car loan,

105
00:05:02,631 --> 00:05:04,190
the bank looks at exactly this.

106
00:05:04,190 --> 00:05:07,753
Now the biggest question. Bhai is paying money every month.

107
00:05:07,753 --> 00:05:10,021
Then why does the bill never finish?

108
00:05:10,021 --> 00:05:12,288
The answer is hidden in this,

109
00:05:12,288 --> 00:05:14,880
where the money you pay actually goes.

110
00:05:14,880 --> 00:05:16,643
When you pay the minimum due,

111
00:05:16,643 --> 00:05:19,582
the bank first cuts its own share. First tax, then fees,

112
00:05:19,582 --> 00:05:21,052
then interest. Whatever is left,

113
00:05:21,052 --> 00:05:23,109
only that reduces the actual debt.

114
00:05:23,109 --> 00:05:24,873
The actual debt is called the principal,

115
00:05:24,873 --> 00:05:26,930
meaning what you originally spent.

116
00:05:26,930 --> 00:05:30,053
The minimum due is small, and the interest share is big.

117
00:05:30,053 --> 00:05:33,522
So the principal drops only a little every month.

118
00:05:33,522 --> 00:05:35,951
It's like you're running on a treadmill.

119
00:05:35,951 --> 00:05:39,420
You're sweating fully, but you stay in the same place.

120
00:05:39,420 --> 00:05:42,119
Some banks also tell you this in the statement,

121
00:05:42,119 --> 00:05:45,156
If you only pay the minimum, when will the bill ever end?

122
00:05:45,156 --> 00:05:48,193
For the first time, my brother read that line carefully.

123
00:05:48,193 --> 00:05:51,230
The answer wasn't in months. The answer was in years.

124
00:05:51,230 --> 00:05:54,812
Think about it: a phone that will become old in a few years.

125
00:05:54,812 --> 00:05:58,751
Its debt may last even longer. A new phone will come,

126
00:05:58,751 --> 00:06:02,333
and the installments of the old phone will still be running.

127
00:06:02,333 --> 00:06:04,840
This is the real trap of the minimum due.

128
00:06:04,840 --> 00:06:09,231
And in the meantime, life doesn't stop. Next month, Mummy's medicine,

129
00:06:09,231 --> 00:06:11,626
school fees, a wedding gift.

130
00:06:11,626 --> 00:06:14,021
A little more went onto the card.

131
00:06:14,021 --> 00:06:16,816
A new bill piled on top of the old bill.

132
00:06:16,816 --> 00:06:19,610
Now even the minimum due started looking bigger.

133
00:06:19,610 --> 00:06:22,220
Mummy put down the tea and asked, beta,

134
00:06:22,220 --> 00:06:24,831
where is the money going every month?

135
00:06:24,831 --> 00:06:28,187
Bhai had no answer. His salary came in,

136
00:06:28,187 --> 00:06:31,543
and went into the card in the first week.

137
00:06:31,543 --> 00:06:33,780
Slowly, the savings ran out.

138
00:06:33,780 --> 00:06:35,376
This is the biggest cost.

139
00:06:35,376 --> 00:06:37,611
The interest isn't just going to the bank.

140
00:06:37,611 --> 00:06:39,526
It is eating your savings.

141
00:06:39,526 --> 00:06:41,760
The money that could have gone into a SIP,

142
00:06:41,760 --> 00:06:43,356
could have become an emergency fund,

143
00:06:43,356 --> 00:06:45,910
is being blown away as interest every month.

144
00:06:45,910 --> 00:06:49,173
So where did the mistake happen? The mistake wasn't buying the phone.

145
00:06:49,173 --> 00:06:52,074
The mistake was thinking the minimum due means paying the bill.

146
00:06:52,074 --> 00:06:54,249
Minimum due is not paying the bill.

147
00:06:54,249 --> 00:06:56,425
It only tells the bank,

148
00:06:56,425 --> 00:06:58,600
"I haven't run away yet."

149
00:06:58,600 --> 00:07:02,203
Now how do you get out? The simplest way: pay the full bill,

150
00:07:02,203 --> 00:07:04,364
every time. Use the card only as much

151
00:07:04,364 --> 00:07:06,886
as you can pay off by the end of the month.

152
00:07:06,886 --> 00:07:11,930
Then that 50-day free credit really works in your favour.

153
00:07:11,930 --> 00:07:15,322
If the bill is big and you can't pay it all at once,

154
00:07:15,322 --> 00:07:18,376
then convert it into EMI. Talk to the bank,

155
00:07:18,376 --> 00:07:22,107
or look for the option in the app. EMI means a fixed installment for a fixed time.

156
00:07:22,107 --> 00:07:25,500
The interest is often lower, and you know in advance when it will end.

157
00:07:25,500 --> 00:07:27,594
There is one more way: balance transfer.

158
00:07:27,594 --> 00:07:30,735
That means shifting the old bill to another card,

159
00:07:30,735 --> 00:07:32,828
where the interest is lower for some time.

160
00:07:32,828 --> 00:07:35,271
Read its fees and terms carefully.

161
00:07:35,271 --> 00:07:39,110
Otherwise you'll get out of one trap and land in a new one.

162
00:07:39,110 --> 00:07:42,974
Or else take a personal loan and pay off the whole card bill in one go.

163
00:07:42,974 --> 00:07:45,352
Interest on a personal loan isn't low either,

164
00:07:45,352 --> 00:07:47,433
but it's often lighter than the card.

165
00:07:47,433 --> 00:07:51,000
And after the loan, don't start running up the card again.

166
00:07:51,000 --> 00:07:53,661
One small habit also helps.

167
00:07:53,661 --> 00:07:56,986
Set the card's auto-debit for the full bill, not the minimum.

168
00:07:56,986 --> 00:08:00,312
My sister does exactly this. For her, the card is a convenience, not a debt.

169
00:08:00,312 --> 00:08:03,970
This is not investment advice, just plain simple math.

170
00:08:03,970 --> 00:08:07,583
The moral of the story: the minimum due is a convenience,

171
00:08:07,583 --> 00:08:10,835
not a way out. It saves you from today's trouble,

172
00:08:10,835 --> 00:08:13,726
but it makes tomorrow's bill bigger.

173
00:08:13,726 --> 00:08:17,700
The day paying the full bill feels hard, change your path, not just press a button.

174
00:08:17,700 --> 00:08:20,288
In the next video we'll open up one more trap,

175
00:08:20,288 --> 00:08:23,616
one you see everywhere this festive season.

176
00:08:23,616 --> 00:08:27,683
No cost EMI. Is it really no cost?

177
00:08:27,683 --> 00:08:31,750
Until then, read your card statement carefully, especially the small print.

178
00:08:31,750 --> 00:08:34,592
Now you tell me, answer directly in the comments.

179
00:08:34,592 --> 00:08:38,652
Have you ever paid only the minimum due? Yes or no,

180
00:08:38,652 --> 00:08:42,712
just write that. If you know someone's story like this,

181
00:08:42,712 --> 00:08:45,960
then write that too, it will help others.
